/**
 * @defgroup pump_efficiency_calculator Achievable Average and Maximum Pump Efficiency
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief HI 2020 achievable efficiency estimates with explicit reference limits.
 * @details
 * What average and maximum efficiencies are generally achievable for this pump type and duty?
 * Use the estimates to compare opportunities when measured or manufacturer performance data are
 * unavailable. They do not establish the actual condition or suitability of an installed pump.
 *
 * @heading{Final Results and Interpretation}
 * Both outputs are dimensionless fractions. The average includes viscosity and speed corrections;
 * the maximum applies the positive relative deviation to that average.
 *
 * @formula{hi2020-pump-results;
 * \eta_{average}=\frac{E_{pre}}{100}C_{\nu}-C_{Ns},\qquad
 * \eta_{max}=\eta_{average}\left(1+\frac{d_{positive}(Q)}{100}\right)
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta_{average}; Corrected average efficiency fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{\eta_{max}; Maximum efficiency estimate; \unitless}
 * @symrow{E_{pre}; HI base efficiency; \percent}
 * @symrow{100; Percentage scale per fractional quantity; \percent}
 * @symrow{C_{\nu}; Viscosity multiplier; \unitless}
 * @symrow{C_{Ns}; Fractional specific-speed decrement; \unitless}
 * @symrow{d_{positive}; Positive relative deviation at flow Q; \percent}
 * @symrow{Q; Liquid flow in the US gallon convention; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Follow @ref optimal_pre_pump_efficiency_calculator, @ref optimal_pump_efficiency_calculator,
 * @ref optimal_specific_speed_calculator, @ref optimal_specific_speed_correction_calculator,
 * and @ref optimal_deviation_factor_calculator for the supporting data and relationships.
 * Interpolation is on raw numerical flow/speed, using @ref pchip_interpolation with no extrapolation.
 * Intermediate values are not rounded. There is no additional viscosity adjustment in the maximum.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Example}
 * For an ANSI/API pump at 2000 rpm, Q=2000 US gpm, H=137 ft, one stage and nu=1.107 cSt,
 * the corrected average is approximately 0.8398357 (83.98357%). The positive deviation is about
 * 3.27343%, giving a multiplier of about 1.0327343. The maximum is approximately 0.8673271
 * (86.73271%). These are empirical estimates; maximum does not mean a guaranteed physical limit.
 *
 * @heading{Availability and Proper Use}
 * Flow must be within the selected base table and positive-deviation table, and specific speed
 * must be within the selected correction table. Endpoints are included. Flow/speed limits are
 * available from pump_efficiency::getLimits; unsupported styles have supported=false and NaN bounds.
 * Axial flow uses V's base curve and nonvertical speed correction. Total head is divided by stages
 * only in the specific-speed definition; no double-suction flow-halving adjustment is introduced.
 *
 * Both results are NaN when a required estimate is unavailable, including nonfinite inputs,
 * nonpositive rpm/head/stages, negative viscosity and unsupported pump types. Do not substitute zero,
 * extrapolate or clamp inputs to fabricate an estimate. A supplied efficiency can support a valid
 * assessment outside HI estimation ranges. Specified-efficiency and PD styles use those separate paths.
 * No output clamping is applied; a finite nonpositive efficiency is not usable for power calculations.
 *
 * The data are the engineering HI 20.3-2020 reference revision dated 2026-09-23. The digitized C/H
 * curves and retained axial mapping require engineering confirmation before product release.
 *
 * @see pump_efficiency::calculate
 */
